blueprint metalcraft-code
  title Remote Coding Workspaces
  tagline a rust api turns github repos into live sprites.dev coding boxes
  stamp edition 2024 · rust 1.91 · vite react

stat Runtime = 2 Railway services from one image
stat Binaries = metalcraft-code · codeworker · migrate
stat API = 21 routes under /api/v1 plus /health and /ready
stat Tables = users · github_installations · workspaces · runs
stat Workspaces = one sprites.dev sprite per workspace at /workspace/app
stat Tokens = GitHub installation tokens minted on demand, never stored

group callers "Callers"
  note browser humans and agent tools hit the same origin

ext agent_pod "metalcraft-agent"
  is the tool-calling pod that drives remote code through HTTP
  why >
    It calls the API with a Bearer mck_ token rather than mounting a repo locally.
    The README names the mcode_* tool pack as the intended caller, which is why the
    API exposes file, exec, git, build and smoke-test operations as first-class routes.
  src README.md
  uses metalcraft-agent

ext browser_user "Browser user"
  is the human operator using the embedded web app
  why >
    The browser flow is not separate from the agent flow. It authenticates through
    the shared Metalcraft ID session cookie, connects GitHub, creates workspaces,
    and then uses the same JSON API the agent uses.
  src frontend/src/routes/Home.tsx frontend/src/routes/WorkspaceDetail.tsx
  uses react

app spa "React workspace UI" x1.2
  is the Vite SPA for GitHub connection, workspace list and in-browser editing
  why >
    It has only two routes, / and /w/:id. The detail page is a small remote IDE:
    file tree, textarea editor, command box and git buttons, all backed by
    same-origin fetch calls with credentials included.
  src frontend/src/App.tsx frontend/src/routes/Home.tsx frontend/src/routes/WorkspaceDetail.tsx frontend/src/lib/api.ts
  uses react react-router-dom vite tailwindcss
  list Routes = /, /w/:id

entry static_spa "Embedded SPA fallback"
  is Axum's rust-embed fallback serving frontend/dist
  why >
    The Docker build compiles Vite first, then rust-embed bakes the dist directory
    into the Rust binary. Unknown paths return index.html, so deep links such as
    /w/:id survive a reload without a separate web server.
  src backend/src/static_assets.rs Dockerfile
  uses rust-embed mime_guess
  fact Folder = ../frontend/dist/

group edge "API edge"
  note one Axum router, auth extracted per protected route

entry api_server "Axum API server" x1.6
  is the metalcraft-code binary: REST API plus embedded SPA
  why >
    main.rs loads Config, builds a lazy Postgres pool, creates AppState and binds
    dual-stack [::]:PORT with an IPv4 fallback for Railway. build_router owns every
    route, then falls through to the SPA asset handler.
  src backend/src/main.rs backend/src/lib.rs backend/src/controllers/api_v1.rs
  uses axum tower-http tokio sqlx reqwest
  num API routes = 21
  env DATABASE_URL PORT BASE_URL RUST_LOG

svc auth_bridge "Auth bridge" x1.2
  is Principal extraction from mc_session cookies or Bearer mck_ PATs
  why >
    Browser sessions are verified offline against the hub JWKS. PATs are
    introspected at the hub /verify endpoint and carry scopes; mutating routes
    call require_write, while a session gets full owner access.
  src backend/src/middleware/auth.rs backend/src/services/hub.rs
  uses axum-extra jsonwebtoken reqwest
  fact Cookie = mc_session
  fact PAT prefix = mck_
  env HUB_BASE_URL HUB_INTERNAL_URL

svc github_flow "GitHub install flow"
  is the browser callback that stores GitHub App installation metadata
  why >
    /github/install sends the user to github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new.
    The callback trusts the session, not the query state, resolves the installation
    through an App JWT, and stores only account metadata plus installation_id.
  src backend/src/controllers/github.rs
  uses sqlx
  list Routes = GET /api/v1/github/install, GET /api/v1/github/callback, GET /api/v1/github/installations, GET /api/v1/github/repos

ext hub "Metalcraft ID hub"
  is the shared identity service for sessions and PAT introspection
  why >
    It owns the mc_session cookie and the /verify endpoint for mck_ personal
    access tokens. Metalcraft Code mirrors users locally after successful auth
    but does not issue identity itself.
  src README.md backend/src/services/hub.rs backend/src/middleware/auth.rs
  uses jwks jwt

lib github_app "GitHub App client" x1.2
  is RS256 App JWT signing and short-lived installation token minting
  why >
    It calls GitHub App endpoints directly with reqwest. Tokens are scoped to
    contents:write and workflows:write, optionally narrowed to the workspace repo,
    and are passed to git through a credential helper instead of being embedded in URLs.
  src backend/src/services/github_app.rs
  uses jsonwebtoken reqwest serde
  fact API = https://api.github.com
  fact Token lifetime = about 1 hour
  env GITHUB_APP_ID GITHUB_APP_SLUG GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH GITHUB_APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET

ext github_api "GitHub API"
  is the external App, repo and git authority
  why >
    It supplies installation metadata, repository listings, installation access
    tokens and commit refs. The service needs Contents read/write, Workflows
    read/write and Metadata read-only permissions.
  src README.md docs/GITHUB_APP_SETUP.md
  uses github

svc workspace_api "Workspace lifecycle API" x1.3
  is create, list, delete, hibernate and wake for workspace rows
  why >
    Creating a workspace only inserts a queued row; cloning is deliberately a later
    route so provisioning cannot wedge on a slow repository. Hibernate takes a
    sprites.dev checkpoint when possible, and wake restores it before marking ready.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspaces.rs backend/src/models/workspace.rs
  uses sqlx uuid
  list Statuses = queued, provisioning, ready, hibernated, failed, deleted
  list Routes = GET|POST /api/v1/workspaces, GET|DELETE /api/v1/workspaces/{id}, POST /api/v1/workspaces/{id}/hibernate, POST /api/v1/workspaces/{id}/wake

svc ops_api "Workspace ops API" x1.8
  is file, exec, git, build, test, actions, expose and run-history routes
  why >
    This is the agent-facing surface. Dynamic paths, command text, file content,
    commit messages and git tokens are base64-encoded before they reach the in-sprite
    shell, and command output is clamped before it is returned or recorded.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs backend/src/models/run.rs
  uses base64 serde sqlx
  num Max file read = 1 MiB
  num Max output = 256 KiB
  list Routes = files, exec, git, build, test, actions, expose, runs

svc run_recorder "Run recorder"
  is the audit writer for clone, exec, git, build, test and actions
  why >
    record_run writes a runs row with kind, command, exit code, output and timing.
    The API exposes list/get routes so an agent or browser can recover what happened
    after a long command finishes.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs backend/src/models/run.rs
  uses sqlx
  list Kinds = exec, clone, build, test, git, actions

group orchestration "Workspace orchestration"
  note queued rows become sprites; ready sprites receive commands

worker codeworker "Codeworker" x1.4
  is the second Railway service that provisions and reaps workspaces
  why >
    It polls the database every WORKER_POLL_SECS, claims one queued row using
    FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, creates a sprite, prepares /workspace/app, and marks
    the row ready. A sibling reaper fails stale provisioning rows and hibernates
    idle ready workspaces every minute.
  src backend/src/bin/codeworker.rs backend/src/worker/mod.rs
  uses tokio sqlx
  fact Claim = FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
  fact Reaper period = 60 s
  env WORKER_POLL_SECS WORKSPACE_IDLE_MINUTES WORKSPACE_STALE_MINUTES SPRITES_TOKEN

lib sprites_client "Sprites REST client" x1.3
  is a thin reqwest wrapper over api.sprites.dev/v1
  why >
    It creates and deletes sprites, runs bash through the non-TTY exec endpoint,
    starts detached commands for build/test, checkpoints disks, restores checkpoints,
    and flips URL privacy for smoke tests.
    The client deliberately keeps the API envelope parsing defensive because the
    sprites.dev list response has shifted across versions.
  src backend/src/services/sprites.rs
  uses reqwest serde
  fact Base URL = https://api.sprites.dev/v1
  list Endpoints = /sprites, /sprites/{name}/exec, /sprites/{name}/checkpoints, /sprites/{name}/checkpoints/{id}/restore
  env SPRITES_TOKEN

ext sprites_dev "sprites.dev"
  is the external workspace compute platform
  why >
    Metalcraft Code treats sprites.dev as the machine provider, not as a code host.
    Each workspace gets one private sprite by default; public exposure is temporary
    and explicit for smoke tests.
  src README.md backend/src/services/sprites.rs
  uses sprites.dev

server live_sprite "Live sprite workspace" x1.3
  is one ephemeral Linux box with the repo at /workspace/app
  why >
    The sprite holds the mutable working tree. File reads, writes, arbitrary bash,
    git commands and detached build/test logs all execute there, while the database
    stores only metadata and audit output.
  src backend/src/services/sprites.rs backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs
  uses bash git
  fact Workdir = /workspace/app
  fact Name = mc-code-<workspace uuid prefix>

lib shell_jail "Shell jail"
  is path validation plus in-sprite realpath checks around shell scripts
  why >
    validate_rel_path rejects absolute paths and any .. segment before a request is
    sent. The script still runs realpath inside the sprite and aborts if the target
    escapes /workspace/app, so the API does not rely on client-side normalization alone.
  src backend/src/util.rs backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs
  uses base64 realpath bash
  fact Workdir = /workspace/app

svc git_ops "Git operations"
  is clone, status, diff, commit, push and pull with App installation tokens
  why >
    A workspace either pins an installation_id or falls back to the user's newest
    installation. Tokens are minted just before use, redacted from returned output,
    and supplied through a temporary credential helper.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs backend/src/services/github_app.rs
  uses git github
  list Ops = clone, status, diff, commit, push, pull

job build_test "Build and test runner"
  is detached long-running commands with log polling and run rows
  why >
    Build and test share mechanics: start a background script on the sprite, poll a
    log file until the command finishes or BUILD_TIMEOUT expires, then record the
    result. This keeps HTTP calls bounded while allowing slow project commands.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs
  uses bash tokio
  num Timeout = 600 s

svc actions_config "Actions configurator"
  is the route that writes GitHub Actions workflow files in the workspace
  why >
    It writes .github/workflows/<filename> through the same jailed shell path and can
    optionally commit the change. The GitHub App permissions include workflows:write,
    so a later push activates the workflow.
  src backend/src/controllers/workspace_ops.rs
  uses github-actions git
  fact Path = .github/workflows/<filename>

group persistence "Persistence and deployment"
  note metadata in Neon; source code remains inside sprites

db neon "Neon Postgres" x1.4
  is the system of record for users, installations, workspaces and runs
  why >
    The pool is lazy so /health can answer while Neon cold-starts. The schema keeps
    GitHub tokens out of storage; only installation_id and account metadata persist.
    Workspace rows carry status, sprite name, checkpoint id, logs and errors.
  src migrations/001_init.sql backend/src/db.rs backend/src/models
  uses postgres sqlx neon
  list Tables = users, github_installations, workspaces, runs, schema_migrations
  list Indexes = idx_github_installations_user, idx_workspaces_user, idx_workspaces_status, idx_runs_workspace
  env DATABASE_URL

job migrate "Manual migrator"
  is the migrate binary applying migrations/NNN_*.sql in order
  why >
    Deployment does not auto-migrate on boot. railway.toml and the Dockerfile both
    say migrations are run manually with ./migrate against DATABASE_URL, and the
    binary records applied files in schema_migrations.
  src backend/src/bin/migrate.rs migrations/001_init.sql railway.toml Dockerfile
  uses sqlx
  fact Policy = manual, never on boot
  env DATABASE_URL

bucket r2_archive "R2 archive handle" !dormant
  is an optional private S3-compatible bucket wrapper
  why >
    AppState constructs S3Service when S3_* credentials are complete, but the current
    route surface does not yet consume it. The workspace model already has
    snapshot_key and the service has deterministic code/<user>/<workspace>/<label>.tar.zst keys.
  src backend/src/services/s3.rs backend/src/lib.rs backend/src/models/workspace.rs
  uses rust-s3 cloudflare-r2
  fact Key pattern = code/{user}/{workspace}/{label}.tar.zst
  env S3_ENDPOINT S3_REGION S3_BUCKET S3_PATH_STYLE S3_ACCESS_KEY S3_SECRET_KEY

config image_build "Single Docker image"
  is one container build containing the SPA and all three Rust binaries
  why >
    The Dockerfile builds frontend/dist first, copies it into the Rust build context
    for rust-embed, and then copies metalcraft-code, codeworker and migrate into the
    runtime image. Railway runs API and worker as separate services from that image.
  src Dockerfile railway.toml
  uses docker railway node rust
  fact Runtime CMD = ./metalcraft-code

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# connections
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

agent_pod -call-> api_server "tool request"
  carry Bearer mck_ + /api/v1/workspaces/{id}/exec
  why >
    Agent tools call the REST surface directly. If the PAT lacks write scope, mutating
    routes fail before touching the workspace.
  vol 0.8

browser_user -call-> static_spa "web page"
  carry GET / and GET /w/:id
  why >
    Human users load the same binary that serves the API. SPA fallback means the
    server can return index.html for deep links.
  vol 0.5

static_spa -data-> spa "compiled assets"
  carry Assets::get("index.html") and frontend/dist bundles
  why >
    rust-embed turns Vite output into bytes inside the Rust binary. If the build did
    not copy frontend/dist first, the fallback returns 404.
  vol 0.4

browser_user -call-> api_server "session api"
  carry mc_session cookie + GET /api/v1/whoami
  why >
    The browser uses the same API as the agent, but credentials are cookies instead
    of PAT headers. A 401 in the frontend redirects to /login.
  vol 0.6

spa -call-> api_server "json fetches"
  carry fetch('/api/v1/workspaces', { credentials: 'include' })
  why >
    frontend/src/lib/api.ts centralizes every call. Errors unwrap the backend's
    { error } JSON shape and unauthorized responses bounce to the hub login flow.
  vol 0.7

api_server -call-> auth_bridge "principal"
  carry Principal::from_request_parts
  why >
    Protected handlers take Principal as an extractor. Failure returns the uniform
    ApiError JSON response before the handler body runs.
  vol 0.9

auth_bridge -call-> hub "identity check"
  carry GET /.well-known/jwks.json or POST /verify
  why >
    Cookies are verified locally with cached JWKS; PATs are introspected remotely.
    If an unknown kid appears, the JWKS cache is refetched.
  vol 0.7

api_server -call-> github_flow "github routes"
  carry GET /api/v1/github/install and /callback
  why >
    build_router sends the GitHub endpoints to controllers/github.rs. The callback
    needs an authenticated session so installation ownership is tied to the user.
  vol 0.3

github_flow -call-> github_app "app jwt"
  carry GithubApp::get_installation and installation_token
  why >
    The controller delegates signing and GitHub HTTP calls to the service. Misconfigured
    App credentials produce a 503 or 502 rather than storing incomplete rows.
  vol 0.3

github_app -call-> github_api "installation token"
  carry POST /app/installations/{id}/access_tokens
  why >
    Tokens are minted on demand for repo listing and git operations. A failed GitHub
    response becomes a bad gateway error to the API caller.
  vol 0.5

github_flow -write-> neon "installation row"
  carry INSERT INTO github_installations ON CONFLICT (installation_id)
  why >
    The callback records installation ownership and account metadata only. Long-lived
    GitHub credentials are never written to the database.
  vol 0.3

api_server -call-> workspace_api "lifecycle routes"
  carry POST /api/v1/workspaces { repo_full_name, branch, installation_id }
  why >
    The router splits lifecycle from interactive operations. Create writes a queued
    row and returns immediately.
  vol 0.6

workspace_api -write-> neon "workspace state"
  carry INSERT INTO workspaces (... status = 'queued')
  why >
    The database queue is the handoff to the worker. Ownership is enforced by user_id
    on load, list and mutation.
  vol 0.7

neon -data-> codeworker "queued row"
  carry SELECT id FROM workspaces WHERE status = 'queued' FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
  why >
    This is the queue discipline. Multiple workers can run without claiming the same
    workspace, and an empty queue just sleeps for WORKER_POLL_SECS.
  vol 0.6

codeworker -call-> sprites_client "provision sprite"
  carry create_sprite(name) then exec('mkdir -p /workspace/app')
  why >
    Provisioning creates a private sprite and runs a minimal setup script. Slow clone
    operations are intentionally excluded from this path.
  vol 0.5

sprites_client -call-> sprites_dev "sprite api"
  carry POST /v1/sprites and POST /v1/sprites/{name}/exec
  why >
    All compute operations leave through this client. Missing SPRITES_TOKEN lets the
    app boot but causes provisioning to fail cleanly.
  vol 0.8

sprites_dev -spawn-> live_sprite "linux box"
  carry sprite named mc-code-<uuid prefix>
  why >
    sprites.dev owns the actual Linux environment. Metalcraft Code records the sprite
    name and URL so later operations know where to execute.
  vol 0.6

codeworker -write-> neon "ready marker"
  carry UPDATE workspaces SET status = 'ready', logs, last_active_at
  why >
    The worker records success only after the setup marker appears in command output.
    Failures write status failed, append logs, and best-effort delete the sprite.
  vol 0.5

workspace_api -call-> sprites_client "sleep wake delete"
  carry checkpoint(), restore(checkpoint_id), delete_sprite(name)
  why >
    Lifecycle routes and the reaper use sprites.dev checkpoints for hibernation.
    Wake verifies the sprite still exists before marking the workspace ready.
  vol 0.4

api_server -call-> ops_api "agent operations"
  carry POST /api/v1/workspaces/{id}/{exec,git,build,test,actions}
  why >
    The high-volume API surface is isolated in workspace_ops.rs. Every handler loads
    the owned workspace and requires ready status before reaching the live sprite.
  vol 0.9

ops_api -call-> shell_jail "safe script"
  carry validate_rel_path() + jail_prelude()
  why >
    File and workflow operations are checked before and inside the sprite. This is
    the defense against path traversal and shell injection from remote callers.
  vol 0.7

shell_jail -call-> sprites_client "exec script"
  carry SpritesClient::exec(sprite, bash -lc script)
  why >
    The jail builds shell snippets; the sprites client transports them. Transport
    failures become HTTP errors rather than partial local writes.
  vol 0.8

ops_api -call-> git_ops "git command"
  carry GitBody { op, message, branch }
  why >
    Git commands need extra token handling, so they are kept behind a dedicated branch
    of workspace_ops.rs. Output is redacted before it leaves the server.
  vol 0.5

git_ops -call-> github_app "repo token"
  carry mint_git_token(ws) -> installation_token(... repositories)
  why >
    Clone, push and pull need a fresh installation token. If the workspace has a repo,
    the token request is narrowed to that repository name.
  vol 0.5

git_ops -call-> shell_jail "credential helper"
  carry git -C /workspace/app status|diff|commit|push|pull
  why >
    Git runs inside the sprite working tree, not on the API host. The helper supplies
    the token without putting it into the remote URL.
  vol 0.5

ops_api -call-> build_test "long command"
  carry BuildBody { cmd }
  why >
    Build and test are explicit routes because they may outlive a normal request. The
    caller gets a Run row instead of an unbounded HTTP stream.
  vol 0.4

build_test -spawn-> sprites_client "detached exec"
  carry exec_detached(... max_run_after_disconnect = 600s)
  why >
    The command keeps running after the HTTP exec disconnects. Separate short execs
    poll the log file until completion or timeout.
  vol 0.4

ops_api -call-> actions_config "workflow write"
  carry ActionsBody { filename, yaml, commit }
  why >
    This route writes a GitHub Actions workflow into the workspace and can commit it.
    Pushing is separate so activation remains caller-controlled.
  vol 0.2

actions_config -call-> shell_jail "workflow file"
  carry .github/workflows/<filename>
  why >
    The workflow file uses the same path jail and base64 content handling as ordinary
    file writes. Invalid filenames never reach the sprite unchecked.
  vol 0.2

ops_api -call-> run_recorder "audit event"
  carry record_run(kind, cmd, exit_code, output)
  why >
    Clone, exec, git, build, test and actions all become durable audit rows. This is
    the read model behind /runs and /runs/{run_id}.
  vol 0.7

run_recorder -write-> neon "run row"
  carry INSERT INTO runs (workspace_id, kind, cmd, status, exit_code, output)
  why >
    The run table stores bounded output and timings. Without it, a detached build
    would finish with no durable result for the browser or agent to inspect.
  vol 0.7

api_server -read-> neon "readiness probe"
  carry SELECT 1 for GET /ready
  why >
    /health avoids the database, but /ready performs a real query. Railway can know
    whether the server process is alive separately from whether Neon is reachable.
  vol 0.3

migrate -write-> neon "schema changes"
  carry migrations/001_init.sql and schema_migrations
  why >
    The image ships migrate, but boot never runs it. Operators apply migrations
    manually, and applied filenames prevent repeat execution.
  vol 0.1

api_server -call-> r2_archive "optional bucket"
  carry AppState::new -> S3Service::new(config)
  why >
    The current code initializes the handle when credentials are present but has no
    active snapshot routes. The dormant edge is retained so readers notice the planned
    storage hook without mistaking it for live traffic.
  vol 0.1

image_build -data-> static_spa "embedded dist"
  carry COPY --from=frontend /app/frontend/dist ./frontend/dist
  why >
    The Rust compile needs the frontend build output present because rust-embed reads
    it at compile time.
  vol 0.2

image_build -data-> api_server "runtime binary"
  carry target/release/metalcraft-code, codeworker, migrate
  why >
    One Docker image contains all executables. Railway changes the start command to
    choose API or worker behavior.
  vol 0.2

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# narrative
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

tab what "What it does"
  h Remote Coding Workspaces
  p >
    The system turns a GitHub repository into a live machine. A [[browser user|browser_user]]
    or [[metalcraft-agent|agent_pod]] enters through the [[Axum API|api_server]],
    proves identity at the [[auth bridge|auth_bridge]], and creates a row in
    [[Neon Postgres|neon]]. The [[codeworker|codeworker]] notices that row, asks
    [[sprites.dev|sprites_dev]] for a private Linux box, prepares /workspace/app,
    and marks the workspace ready.
  p >
    After that, the hot path is command transport. [[Workspace ops|ops_api]] turns
    file edits, shell commands, git operations, build/test runs and workflow writes
    into guarded scripts for the [[live sprite|live_sprite]]. The API host stores
    metadata and audit output; the mutable source tree stays inside the sprite.
  note >
    The surprising split is clone. Provisioning creates an empty workspace only;
    repository clone is an explicit later operation so a slow GitHub checkout cannot
    block the worker queue.

tab built "How it's built"
  h Boring queue, explicit trust boundaries
  p >
    There is no message broker. The database is the queue: [[workspace rows|neon]]
    move queued → provisioning → ready, and the worker claims them with
    {{skip locked}}. Auth is delegated to {{Metalcraft ID}}; browser cookies verify
    against JWKS and PATs are checked through /verify.
  p >
    GitHub credentials are short-lived. The [[GitHub App client|github_app]] signs
    an App JWT, mints an installation token for the selected repo, hands it to
    [[git operations|git_ops]], and redacts it from output. Long commands go through
    the [[build/test runner|build_test]], which records results in [[run history|run_recorder]].
  p >
    Deployment is one [[Docker image|image_build]] with three binaries. The API serves
    the [[embedded React app|static_spa]], the worker runs separately from the same
    image, and [[migrations|migrate]] are intentionally manual.

term "skip locked" = PostgreSQL's FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED queue pattern: concurrent workers skip rows another worker has claimed
term "Metalcraft ID" = the shared identity hub that issues mc_session cookies and introspects mck_ personal access tokens
